.TH "schema" 3 "Version 0.9.0" "mbedded-cbor" \" -*- nroff -*-
.ad l
.nh
.SH NAME
schema \- structs as Packed CBOR, with fields at fixed offsets
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SS "Classes"

.in +1c
.ti -1c
.RI "struct \fBcbor::tag\fP"
.br
.RI "The tag number of a struct, in the member \fR\fBtag\fP\fP of its \fBcbor::describe_members\fP specialization\&. "
.ti -1c
.RI "class \fBcbor::typed_array_view< E >\fP"
.br
.RI "A random access, sized range over an RFC 8746 typed array in the message\&. "
.ti -1c
.RI "class \fBcbor::schema< T >::accessor< U, Verified >\fP"
.br
.RI "Reads the part \fRU\fP of a message of \fRT\fP\&. "
.ti -1c
.RI "class \fBcbor::schema< T >::accessor< fixed_element< S >, Verified >\fP"
.br
.RI "Reads one element of type \fRS\fP in a vector of fixed-size structs\&. "
.in -1c
.SS "Enumerations"

.in +1c
.ti -1c
.RI "enum class \fBcbor::description_place\fP { \fBinside\fP, \fBapart\fP }"
.br
.RI "Tells if a message holds the description of its structs (\fRinside\fP) or not (\fRapart\fP)\&. "
.in -1c
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP 
\fRcbor::schema<T>\fP writes a struct as one Packed CBOR message (draft-ietf-cbor-packed-19)\&. Each field of fixed size has an offset that the compiler computes\&. A read goes to that offset\&. It does not walk the fields before it\&. Include \fR<\fBcbor/schema\&.hpp\fP>\fP, or \fR<\fBcbor/cbor\&.hpp\fP>\fP, which includes it\&.

.PP
A decoder of Packed CBOR reads the message as a map\&. The keys of the map are the names of the members\&. \fRcbor::schema<T>\fP reads only a message of \fRT\fP\&. A message of another struct gives \fRincorrect_type\fP\&.

.PP
\fRcbor::schema<T>\fP reads the description of each struct from \fR\fBcbor::describe_members\fP<T>\fP\&. It builds with C++23 on g++ 15, g++ 16, clang 23 and AppleClang\&. With C++26 reflection (g++ 16 with \fR-std=c++26 -freflection\fP), it also checks each description against the public members\&.

.PP
Use \fRcbor::schema\fP for messages that your own programs write and read\&. Use \fR\fBcbor::structs\fP\fP for CBOR that any program writes, mapped into the members of a struct or a class\&.
.SH "THE DESCRIPTION"
.PP
Each struct has one tag\&. Each member has one key, and each key is a text\&. \fRCBOR_DEFINE_TYPE(type, tag, members\&.\&.\&.)\fP writes the description of a struct at global scope\&. \fR\fBCBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS(type, tag, names\&.\&.\&.)\fP\fP writes it from the names of the members\&. Each name is the key of its member\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fBCBOR_MEMBER(type, member)\fP\fP takes the name of the member as the key\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fBcbor::member\fP{"x", &type::member}\fP takes the text \fRx\fP as the key\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
The macros write a specialization of \fR\fBcbor::describe_members\fP\fP\&. \fR\fBcbor::structs\fP\fP reads the same specialization\&.
.PP

.PP
These do not compile:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
a struct with no description, or with \fRcbor::untagged\fP as its tag;
.IP "\(bu" 2
a struct in an anonymous namespace that no code uses\&. clang refuses it with \fR-Wunused-const-variable\fP;
.IP "\(bu" 2
two structs of \fRT\fP with one tag number, the alternatives of a variant included;
.IP "\(bu" 2
a tag number that a standard type uses: 0, 1, 2, 3, 24, 64 to 87, 100, 258, 1004 and 32873;
.IP "\(bu" 2
a tag number that the IANA registry calls always invalid: 21334, 65535, 4294967295 and 18446744073709551615;
.IP "\(bu" 2
a key that is not a text;
.IP "\(bu" 2
two members with one key;
.IP "\(bu" 2
a member that the description lists twice;
.IP "\(bu" 2
a member that the description does not list\&. With reflection, the check uses the public members\&. Without reflection, Boost\&.PFR counts the members of an aggregate where it is available, and a count that differs does not compile\&.
.PP

.PP
\fRcbor::tags_registered<T>()\fP is true when the tag numbers of the structs in \fRT\fP differ\&.

.PP
A class with a described base is not an aggregate that takes an allocator\&.

.PP
A type whose name holds a comma, such as \fRpair<int, int>\fP, takes an alias in the macro\&. The preprocessor splits the arguments of a macro at each comma\&.
.SH "CLASSES WITH A BASE CLASS"
.PP
A class with a base class is described with its base\&. Use \fRCBOR_DEFINE_DERIVED_TYPE_MEMBERS(type, base, tag, members\&.\&.\&.)\fP, or \fRusing base = B;\fP in \fR\fBcbor::describe_members\fP<T>\fP\&. The description then lists the members of the base too\&.

.PP
With reflection, the named base must be the one direct base of the class\&. A base class that the description does not name does not compile\&. Without reflection, the named base is accepted when it is a base class of the type\&. The check also accepts an indirect base\&.
.SH "THE VARIANT MEMBERS"
.PP
A \fRstd::variant\fP member of a described class takes these alternatives:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
a class with a description and a tag;
.IP "\(bu" 2
a number, a \fRbool\fP, or an enum with a fixed underlying type;
.IP "\(bu" 2
a text or a byte string\&.
.PP

.PP
A list, a map, an array, an optional, a variant, and \fRstd::monostate\fP do not compile as alternatives\&. A list, a map, an array or an optional of a variant does not compile\&. Each alternative occurs once\&.

.PP
The CBOR head of an item chooses its alternative\&. Two alternatives whose items begin with one head do not compile\&. Examples are \fRstd::int32_t\fP with \fRstd::uint32_t\fP, \fRfloat\fP with \fRstd::bfloat16_t\fP, an enum with its underlying type, and two text types\&. The class alternatives name one direct base class, and it is the same base for each of them\&.
.SH "WRITE A MESSAGE"
.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRencode(value)\fP gives a \fRstd::expected<std::string, \fBcbor::error\fP>\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRencode(value, target)\fP appends the message to \fRtarget\fP\&. It gives the count of bytes that it appended\&. \fRtarget\fP is a \fRstd::string\fP, a container of \fRchar\fP, a \fRstd::span<char>\fP or a message writer\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
A \fRstd::span<char>\fP that is shorter than the message gives \fRno_buffer_space\fP\&.
.PP

.PP
Only public members are written\&. Each field has a head of fixed width, so a value does not change the offset of a field\&. A float is written at the width of its type, and its bits are copied\&.

.PP
A list of fixed-size structs is written with one copy where the struct in memory has the bytes of the element\&. A compile-time probe finds the layout\&. Otherwise each element is written field by field\&.
.SH "READ ONE VALUE"
.PP
\fRcbor::schema<T>::at_path<Path>(bytes, indexes\&.\&.\&.)\fP reads one value with no accessor\&. The value is a number, a \fRbool\fP, an enum, or a \fRstd::optional\fP of one\&. It gives a \fRstd::expected\fP of the value\&.

.PP
A path starts with \fR$\fP\&. A part \fR\&.name\fP names a member\&. A part \fR[n]\fP names an element\&. A part \fR[]\fP takes its index from the next argument, a \fRstd::size_t\fP\&. The count of arguments equals the count of \fR[]\fP\&. The path is not JSONPath\&. It has no wildcard, no filter and no descendant segment\&.

.PP
On a byte string, a \fRstd::array\fP or a \fRstd::vector\fP of \fRstd::uint8_t\fP or \fRstd::byte\fP, \fR[n]\fP reads one byte\&.

.PP
These do not compile: a member that \fRT\fP does not have, a wrong count of arguments, a path to a text, a constant index past the end of a \fRstd::array\fP, and a path to a member that is not trivially copyable, such as a \fRstd::vector\fP of structs\&.
.SH "OPEN A MESSAGE"
.PP
\fRcbor::schema<T>::path(\&.\&.\&.)\fP opens a message and gives the root accessor, \fRcbor::schema<T>::accessor<T>\fP\&. It checks the message once, and it reads no field\&. The forms are:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpath(std::move(bytes))\fP: the string moves into the owner\&. Nothing is copied\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpath(owner)\fP: \fRowner\fP is a \fRstd::shared_ptr<std::string const>\fP\&. The message is read in place\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpath(owner, part)\fP: \fRpart\fP is a \fRstd::string_view\fP into memory that \fRowner\fP, a \fRstd::shared_ptr<void const>\fP, keeps alive\&.
.PP

.PP
An accessor holds its owner\&. Each value and each accessor that it gives holds the same owner\&. So a child accessor stays valid after the root accessor ends\&.
.SH "READ WITH AN ACCESSOR"
.PP
\fRat_path<Path>(indexes\&.\&.\&.)\fP of an accessor reads a part of the message\&. A path that starts with \fR$\fP starts at the root\&. A path that starts with \fR@\fP starts at the part that the accessor names\&. The result is a \fRstd::expected\fP:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
a number, a \fRbool\fP or an enum gives the value;
.IP "\(bu" 2
a text or a byte string gives a \fRstd::string_view\fP into the message;
.IP "\(bu" 2
a struct, a list or a map gives its accessor;
.IP "\(bu" 2
a \fRstd::optional\fP of a type E gives a \fRstd::optional\fP of the result of E\&.
.PP

.PP
\fRsize()\fP gives the count of a list or a map\&. \fRview<Path>()\fP gives a \fR\fBcbor::typed_array_view\fP\fP of a list of numbers\&. Only the root accessor has \fRview\fP\&.

.PP
A \fRstd::string_view\fP stays valid while an accessor of the same owner lives\&. Keep the accessor in a variable\&. \fRat_path\fP on a temporary accessor does not compile\&. A call on an accessor that was moved from throws \fRstd::logic_error\fP\&.

.PP
A \fRstd::variant\fP member has \fRindex()\fP, which gives the index of the held alternative, and \fRget<A>()\fP\&. \fRget<A>()\fP gives a class alternative as its accessor\&. It gives a number, \fRbool\fP or enum as its value\&. It gives a text or a byte string as a \fRstd::string_view\fP\&. Another alternative gives \fRincorrect_type\fP\&. A path cannot end at a variant member\&.

.PP
A list of fixed-size structs gives a random access range of element accessors\&. In an element, \fR@\fP reads a member of the element\&. \fR$\fP starts at the root\&.
.SH "READ A LIST OF NUMBERS"
.PP
\fRtyped_array_view<E>\fP is a random access, sized range over a list of numbers in the message\&. Each element is a value of \fRE\fP, read in little endian\&. An element is a value, not a reference\&. The view holds the owner of the bytes\&. It has \fRsize\fP, \fRempty\fP, \fRbegin\fP, \fRend\fP, \fRrbegin\fP, \fRrend\fP, \fRfront\fP, \fRback\fP, \fRfirst\fP, \fRlast\fP, \fRat\fP and \fRoperator[]\fP\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRat\fP, \fRfirst\fP and \fRlast\fP give an index error as a value\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRoperator[]\fP, \fRfront\fP and \fRback\fP throw \fRstd::logic_error\fP outside the view\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRbegin\fP and \fRend\fP on a temporary view do not compile\&.
.PP
.SH "READ THE WHOLE STRUCT"
.PP
\fRcbor::schema<T>::decode(\&.\&.\&.)\fP reads the whole message into a \fRT\fP\&. It gives a \fRstd::expected<\fBcbor::ref\fP<T>, \fBcbor::error\fP>\fP\&. The \fR\fBcbor::ref\fP\fP holds the owner of the bytes, and no form copies the bytes\&. An rvalue \fRstd::string\fP moves into a new owner\&. A \fRstd::shared_ptr<std::string const>\fP is read in place\&. A \fRstd::shared_ptr<void const>\fP with a view of the bytes is read in place too\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
A struct with a default constructor is made with it\&. Then each described member is set\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
A class with no default constructor is made by a constructor\&. That constructor takes the described members in the order of the description\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
The forms with a \fRtarget\fP take the start value\&. A \fRstd::pmr\fP container with its memory resource gives the result its memory\&. These forms need a default constructor of \fRT\fP\&.
.PP
.SH "STORE THE DESCRIPTION APART"
.PP
Each call takes \fR\fBcbor::description_place\fP\fP as a template argument\&. The default is \fRinside\fP, and the message holds the description of its structs\&.

.PP
With \fRcbor::description_place::apart\fP, \fRencode\fP writes the message without the description\&. Each read form reads such a message only with \fRapart\fP\&. A read with \fRapart\fP of a message that has the description gives \fRincorrect_type\fP\&.

.PP
\fRcbor::schema<T>::description()\fP gives the description\&. It is the same in every message of \fRT\fP\&. The size of a message written with \fRapart\fP plus the size of \fRdescription()\fP is the size of the message with the description\&. A generic decoder of Packed CBOR does not read a message written with \fRapart\fP\&.
.SH "READ A MESSAGE FROM A PEER YOU TRUST"
.PP
\fRunverified_path\fP, \fRunverified_at_path\fP and \fRunverified_decode\fP take the same arguments as \fRpath\fP, \fRat_path\fP and \fRdecode\fP\&. They check each bound of the message, so no read leaves the message\&. They do not check the data of the message\&. A wrong byte gives a wrong value, not an error\&.

.PP
These forms do not check:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
the tag of each struct;
.IP "\(bu" 2
the head of a number, a \fRbool\fP, a fixed string or a \fRstd::array\fP;
.IP "\(bu" 2
the head byte of a text, a list or a map, and the tag of a typed array;
.IP "\(bu" 2
the prefix of a list of fixed-size structs;
.IP "\(bu" 2
the start of the table, and the fillers before the items;
.IP "\(bu" 2
that the length of a typed array is a multiple of its element size;
.IP "\(bu" 2
that a \fRbool\fP byte is 0 or 1\&. Any other byte reads as \fRtrue\fP;
.IP "\(bu" 2
in \fRunverified_decode\fP, the order of the references and of the items\&.
.PP

.PP
These forms check:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
that each head, length and item lies inside the message;
.IP "\(bu" 2
that each directory entry lies between the start and the end of the items;
.IP "\(bu" 2
each index against the count of its list, table or directory;
.IP "\(bu" 2
the limits of the build and, in \fRunverified_decode\fP, the nesting depth\&.
.PP

.PP
Every accessor of \fRunverified_path\fP is unverified too\&.
.SH "SIZE AND OFFSET"
.PP
\fRcbor::schema<T>::fixed_size()\fP gives the count of bytes of the root record of \fRT\fP\&. The root record ends the message\&. \fRfixed_size<U>()\fP gives the count of bytes that a field of type \fRU\fP takes\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
A number takes its head and its argument, both of fixed width\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
A struct takes its class tag, its table reference, a record head of 5 bytes and its fields\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
A text, a list, a map, an optional of variable size, or a \fRstd::variant\fP takes a reference \fR6(N)\fP of 6 bytes\&.
.PP

.PP
A type with no fixed size does not compile\&. \fRfixed_size\fP gives no error at run time\&.

.PP
\fR\fBcbor::item_size\fP\fP of a message gives the size of the message\&.

.PP
\fRcbor::schema<T>::member_offset<&T::m>()\fP gives the offset of the head of the member \fRm\fP from the start of its record\&. The fields follow the key order of RFC 8949, section 4\&.2\&.1: a shorter key comes first, then the bytes of the key\&. This order is not the order of the declarations\&.
.SH "THE WIRE FORMAT"
.PP
A message is tag 113 (Basic Packed CBOR) on an array of two items: a table and a rump\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
The table holds the record function, tag 114, of each struct\&. The record function gives the keys of one struct\&. For each struct that is the element of a list of fixed-size structs, the table holds a description\&. The description is the class tag and the key and the RFC 8746 type of each member\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
The table holds a directory of 4-byte offsets of the parts of variable size\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
The fillers are \fRundefined\fP items\&. They place the parts at index 16 or later\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
The parts hold the texts, the byte strings, the lists, the maps and the items of the variant members\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
The rump is the root record\&. It stands at the end of the message\&.
.PP

.PP
A record starts with its class tag\&. A reference to its record function follows\&. The first eight references are tags 128 to 135\&. Later references are tag 6 on an array of two\&. Then an array with a count of 4 bytes holds the fields\&. Each field has a head of fixed width\&. A field of variable size is \fR6(N)\fP, a reference with a 4-byte \fRN\fP into the table\&.

.PP
A \fRstd::variant\fP member is a field \fR6(N)\fP\&. Its part is the item of the alternative that the variant holds\&. The head of the item names the alternative\&.

.PP
A list of a number is an RFC 8746 typed array in little endian\&. A \fRstd::vector<double>\fP is tag 86\&.

.PP
A list of fixed-size structs is \fR32873([simple(j), h'\&.\&.\&.'])\fP\&. \fRj\fP is the index of its description in the table\&. From index 16 on, \fR6(N)\fP takes the place of \fRsimple(j)\fP\&. The byte string holds the elements with no padding, in little endian\&. Tag 32873 is provisional\&. The IANA registry does not have it yet\&.
.SH "WHAT IS CHECKED ONCE AND WHAT AT EACH READ"
.PP
\fRpath\fP checks once:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
that the message is at least as long as the table and the root;
.IP "\(bu" 2
that the start of the table holds the keys of \fRT\fP and of each struct inside it, and the descriptions\&. A message of another layout gives \fRincorrect_type\fP;
.IP "\(bu" 2
that the directory lies inside the message;
.IP "\(bu" 2
the tag of the struct of the root\&.
.PP

.PP
With \fRcbor::description_place::apart\fP, the message holds no keys\&. \fRpath\fP then checks the start of the message, the count of the table and the tag of the root\&.

.PP
Each \fRat_path\fP of an accessor checks at each read:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
the index of each step against the count of the list;
.IP "\(bu" 2
that each offset that it reads from the directory lies inside the message;
.IP "\(bu" 2
the tag of each struct on the path;
.IP "\(bu" 2
the head of the field that it reads;
.IP "\(bu" 2
that a part of variable size lies inside the message and does not overlap another part;
.IP "\(bu" 2
for a list of fixed-size structs, the tag 32873 and its reference, and that the length is a multiple of the size of the element\&.
.PP

.PP
\fRview\fP checks the tag and the length of the typed array once\&. After that, each element read checks only its index\&. \fRcbor::schema<T>::at_path\fP makes the checks of \fRpath\fP and of \fRat_path\fP in one call\&.
.SH "COST"
.PP
\fRpath\fP with a shared owner takes a time that does not depend on the size of the message\&. \fRpath\fP with an rvalue takes the same time, and it makes one allocation for the owner\&. Each \fRat_path\fP takes one step for each part of its path\&. \fRdecode\fP and \fRencode\fP take a time in the size of the message\&.

.PP
An error of \fRpath\fP, \fRat_path\fP or \fRdecode\fP can cost a walk over the message\&. The walk checks whether the message ends too early\&.
.SH "ERRORS"
.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRtoo_little_data\fP: the message ends too early\&. \fRpath\fP, \fRat_path\fP and \fRdecode\fP give this error also where another check fails first\&. A message that is shorter than its table and root gives it in \fRpath\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRincorrect_type\fP: a message of another struct or layout, a wrong tag of a struct, a wrong head of a field, a variant item whose head names no alternative, or \fRget<A>()\fP of another alternative\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRindex_out_of_bounds\fP: an index past the end of a list\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRunpopulated_table_index\fP and \fRsyntax_error\fP: a reference or a directory entry that is broken\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRinadmissible_type_for_tag_content\fP: a typed array, or a list of fixed-size structs, whose length is not a multiple of its element size\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRnesting_depth_exceeded\fP: a value that is nested too deep for \fRencode\fP or for \fRdecode\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRnot_enough_memory\fP: an allocation of the owner in \fRpath\fP fails, or an allocation of the result in \fRdecode\fP fails\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRno_buffer_space\fP: a \fRstd::span<char>\fP is shorter than the message\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRvalue_too_large\fP: the message is longer than 2^32 - 1 bytes\&.
.PP

.PP
A null or empty owner throws \fRstd::logic_error\fP in \fRpath\fP, \fRdecode\fP and the unverified forms\&. A call on a moved-from accessor throws it too\&. \fRoperator[]\fP, \fRfront\fP and \fRback\fP of a typed array view throw it outside the view\&.

.PP
The unverified forms give the error of the check that fails\&.

.PP
 
.SH "EXAMPLES"
 
.PP
.nf
    auto bytes = cbor::schema<car>::encode(car{"Volvo", 5, {{17, 2\&.5f}, {18, 2\&.25f}}, {1\&.0, 2\&.0, 3\&.0}});  // the message of a car
    auto const seats = cbor::schema<car>::at_path<"$\&.seats">(*bytes);  // 5, read at its offset
    auto const back = cbor::schema<car>::decode(std::move(*bytes));  // the whole car
    std::string const make = (*back)\->make;  // "Volvo"
.fi
.PP
 
